---
>   - If they do NOT match, first decide WHY, because there are two different situations and they
>     get DIFFERENT treatment:
>     (a) WRONG LOCATION - the text you copied belongs to a different field/entity entirely (e.g.
>         you accidentally read a different person's name, or a different plot's row). Here you
>         really did locate the wrong text: re-scan the page for the correct row/label and fix it,
>         or mark not found if it truly is not there.
>     (b) RIGHT LOCATION, CONFLICTING VALUE - you are confident you read the CORRECT row/label/
>         position on the page (the right person, the right plot, the right date stamp), but the
>         value written there genuinely differs from english_value (e.g. the page says plot 2861
>         where english_value says 2661; the page's amount is 40000 where english_value says
>         50000). This is a real, useful finding, NOT an error on your part - part of this task is
>         specifically to catch cases where the document disagrees with the database. In this case
>         you MUST still report it as a match: set "found": true, keep "odia_text" as the exact
>         text you read (do not blank it out), set "latin_readback" to your honest reading of that
>         text (do NOT force it to match english_value), give a lower "confidence" to reflect the
>         discrepancy, and describe the conflict in "notes" (e.g. "page shows 2861, english_value
>         says 2661"). NEVER use "notes" as a substitute for the field value - if you can read a
>         value in the ink, it belongs in "odia_text"/"latin_readback", with "notes" only
>         describing the discrepancy. Only mark "found": false when the field's location is
>         genuinely illegible or absent in every script, never merely because it disagrees with
>         english_value.
>   Do not report text whose readback contradicts the English due to a genuine mis-location (case
>   a); DO report text whose readback contradicts the English because the source disagrees with
>   the database (case b).
